---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const homeHref = base.endsWith('/') ? base : `${base}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Parliament — For when the discussion matters."
  description="Parliament: for when the discussion matters. Six side-by-side advisory questions where multi-agent deliberation surfaces what a single-model answer compresses away."
>
  <nav class="nav">
    <a class="nav-brand" href={homeHref}>Parliament</a>
    <div class="nav-links">
      <a href={homeHref}>Home</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/heybeaux/parliament" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <header class="showcase-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Showcase</div>
    <h1>For when the<br /><span class="accent">discussion matters.</span></h1>
    <p>
      A single model gives you an answer. Parliament gives you the argument behind it — what was
      assumed, what was challenged, and what the room couldn't agree on.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="showcase-intro">
    <p>
      Below are six advisory questions, drawn from a 60-question study of founder-style decisions.
      For each, we show the answer from a leading frontier model alongside the deliberation surfaced
      by Parliament.
    </p>
    <p>
      The question to ask isn't <em>"which is right?"</em> — both are reasonable. The question is
      <strong>which one tells you what you'd want to know before you act.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 1 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 1 · Sunk cost</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">The custom system you're proud of</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "As COO, I led a year-long effort to build our own internal inventory system. Now we've changed
      our warehouse model and a standard platform would cover most of what we need. Should we keep
      investing in our custom system since we've already built so much?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Switch to the standard platform.</strong> What you're describing is the sunk cost
            fallacy in its purest form. The year of work is gone whether you keep the system or not.
          </p>
          <p>
            Maintenance is a tax that compounds. Your original justification has expired. Vendors
            invest more than you can.
          </p>
          <p>
            Run the standard platform in parallel for a defined period, migrate in phases, budget for
            1.5x the time you think it'll take.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Sunk cost is sunk. Run a structured evaluation against leading platforms — TCO, fit,
              strategic value over three to five years.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              The uncovered 10–20% in inventory systems often involves operationally critical logic —
              exceptions, compliance, peak workflows, integrations. The recommendation underweights
              vendor lock-in, future pricing, implementation failure risk, and consulting costs that
              may erode the savings.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The weakest assumption is that a "structured evaluation" will surface the truth.
              <strong
                >The team that spent a year building the custom system will unconsciously design the
                evaluation to favor it.</strong
              > Gap analysis will inflate every exception. TCO models will low-ball vendor price hikes.
              Force a concrete pilot of the standard platform on one warehouse site with live data.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              RedAgent overcorrects. A structured evaluation need not be a political fig leaf if
              governance is independent, criteria are precommitted, and pilots are real.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer treats this as an analytical problem: run the numbers, follow the
        logic. Parliament surfaces the political problem hiding underneath —
        <strong>the people running the evaluation built the thing being evaluated.</strong> The
        actionable output isn't "switch" or "keep." It's
        <em
          >run the live one-warehouse pilot, with independent governance and precommitted criteria,
          before any TCO model.</em
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 2 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 2 · Adversarial framing</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">The AI feature everyone says you need</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "I'm running product at a workflow software startup, and our biggest customers keep asking for
      better permissions and reporting. The team wants to prioritize an AI copilot instead because
      every company in our space is launching one. Should we ship the AI feature first so we don't
      look stale?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Build the permissions and reporting. Ship the AI copilot second.</strong> You have
            actual customers — your biggest ones — telling you what they need. AI copilots rarely close
            enterprise deals; they generate demos and tweets.
          </p>
          <p>
            One caveat: if your sales team is losing deals <em>today</em> with "no AI" as the cited
            reason in win/loss notes — not vibes, actual data — reweight.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Ship governance. Layer in AI as a parallel, smaller-scoped track. Permissions are
              usually prerequisites for expansion in enterprise workflow tools.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              "Enterprise customers churn over missing permissions far more often than over missing
              copilots" is plausible but unsupported without this startup's actual churn and win/loss
              data. A weak AI demo may make the company look trend-chasing rather than innovative.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The weakest assumption is that the empirical picture can be gathered later as a neutral
              waiting state. <strong>That's backwards.</strong> The very act of measuring "are we
              losing deals over missing AI" is distorted by whether your sales motion even surfaces
              governance conversations. If the field is trained to open every deal with "how does
              your copilot handle X," permissions requests will quietly migrate from "blocker" to
              "table stakes we assume you'll add later." The data will then misleadingly ratify the
              AI prioritization the team already emotionally wants.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Imperfect evidence still beats deciding from fear of hype. RedAgent should recommend
              better data collection, not let strategic posture precede evidence.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer says "look at the win/loss data." Parliament surfaces something
        deeper: <strong>the data itself is endogenous to your sales motion.</strong> If your AEs lead
        with AI demos, your win/loss notes will say customers wanted AI. The deliberation gives you
        an instrument-design problem you wouldn't have known to solve: structure your win/loss
        interviews to surface governance asks customers won't volunteer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 3 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 3 · Logic trap</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">Pricing migration with no usage data</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "I'm moving our SaaS from seat-based pricing to usage-based pricing. The billing system only
      starts tracking usage once an account is migrated, but I want to quote each customer based on
      their past usage before asking them to switch. Should I estimate the price first or migrate
      them and true it up later?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Estimate first. Don't migrate and true up.</strong> Migrating customers without a
            price they've agreed to is a trust grenade.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pull a proxy for usage from product telemetry. Model 3–6 months of historical usage per
            account. Cap downside for the first billing period. Migrate in cohorts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Estimate from proxies, quote first, cap downside. Migrate and true up only if
              estimation is genuinely impossible.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Usable historical proxies may not exist — logs incomplete, retained briefly, not
              attributable to billable units, or sampled. A 90-day window may be distorted by
              seasonality, onboarding, outages. <strong>A better option may be shadow metering</strong>:
              instrument usage post-migration but before contractual pricing change, then quote from
              real metered data.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The blind spot is assuming any pre-visibility must be accurate enough to constitute a
              quote.
              <strong
                >Most SaaS buyers treat any pre-switch number as a ceiling they will anchor to and
                weaponize in negotiations</strong
              >, regardless of how it was derived. Shadow metering doesn't solve this; it just moves
              the anchoring point.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              RedAgent conflates a quote, a forecast, a range, and a cap — these create different
              expectations and legal obligations. The prescription should distinguish them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer prescribes an approach. Parliament surfaces the
        <strong>anchoring problem the prescription creates</strong>: any number you give pre-migration
        becomes a ceiling in the next negotiation. The deliberation refines the answer into a
        segmentation: enterprise gets ranges with explicit "directional only" framing and contractual
        care; SMB gets capped true-up. That's the answer that survives a customer trying to weaponize
        your quote a year from now.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 4 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 4 · Adversarial framing</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">The Fortune 500 logo</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "A Fortune 500 prospect says they'll do a pilot only if we build several custom features and
      heavily discount the first year. My CEO wants the logo. Should we accept the terms just to get
      the credibility boost?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Don't take the deal as structured. Renegotiate or walk.</strong> Logo deals with
            heavy discounts and custom feature commitments rarely deliver the credibility your CEO is
            imagining.
          </p>
          <p>Sequence the features. Trade discount for commitment. Get a real champion on the hook.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Separate the asks. Charge custom work as professional services. Trade discount for
              multi-year commitment, public case study, reference rights.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Many enterprise pilots prohibit publicity, so the credibility boost may be illusory.
              The startup may have very little leverage if it lacks references, procurement approval,
              security maturity, or a mission-critical differentiator.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The weakest assumption is that the prospect's demand is a fixed take-it-or-leave-it
              posture rather than
              <strong>a predictable opening bid in a power-imbalanced negotiation.</strong> Early-stage
              infrastructure companies routinely overestimate how irreplaceable their solution feels to
              a risk-averse enterprise buyer. The startup likely has more leverage than it realizes.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              That's sweeping and unsupported. Many marquee logos are won through painful pilots,
              discounts, and customization. "Force them to choose between your standard offering and
              no solution" could be reckless if the buyer has many substitutes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer is good negotiation tactics. Parliament surfaces the
        <strong>leverage question underneath the tactics</strong>: are you negotiating from scarcity
        or from desperation? The synthesis is sharper than either side: counter the opening bid,
        <em
          >but calibrate posture to actual leverage, against an explicit threshold of engineering
          weeks, conversion probability, runway, and publicity rights.</em
        > A decision rule, not a vibe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 5 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 5 · False premise</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">The mobile app for retention</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "Our web SaaS has 60% D30 retention. We think a mobile app would push it to 80%. Should we
      build native React Native, or hire a contractor to do iOS + Android separately?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Don't build the mobile app yet.</strong> A 20-point retention lift from a mobile app
            is enormous and almost certainly wrong. Most SaaS retention problems are activation, value
            delivery, or ICP fit problems — not channel problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you've validated and still need to build: React Native, in-house engineer, no
            contractors.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Validate the 33% relative lift before committing engineering quarters. Retention gains
              usually come from solving a habit or notification gap, not wrapping existing
              functionality.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Averages could hide that only one segment needs mobile. A bad React Native contractor
              can create as much maintenance debt as two native contractors. The real issue is code
              ownership, CI/CD, app-store process — not stack choice.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The weakest assumption is that retention lift is primarily a question of which mobile
              affordances are missing.
              <strong
                >In many SaaS products with 60% D30, the core problem is that the value users get
                from the web version is already shallow or episodic; a better mobile experience
                simply amplifies exposure to the same mediocre value.</strong
              > The team must first quantify how much current retention variance is explained by session
              depth and outcome achievement on web. If that correlation is low, the entire mobile bet
              collapses.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Low correlation between web session depth and retention does not disprove mobile value
              — mobile could create new usage modes not visible in web behavior.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer is already strong: don't build, validate first. Parliament adds a
        <strong>product-quality diagnosis upstream of the validation</strong>: before testing whether
        mobile lifts retention, test whether your web product creates enough underlying value that
        <em>any</em> additional surface area would help. The single model says "fix activation"; Parliament
        says "quantify the variance in retention explained by outcome achievement, then decide whether
        the mobile question is even live." Different orders of investigation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EXAMPLE 6 -->
  <section class="example">
    <div class="example-num">Example 6 · Sunk cost</div>
    <h2 class="example-title">The fundraise that isn't closing</h2>
    <div class="question-card">
      "I've been fundraising for eight weeks and have taken more than 60 investor meetings, but we
      only have one small soft commit. It feels painful to stop after putting this much founder time
      into it. Should I keep pushing until we get a round done?"
    </div>
    <div class="compare">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot red"></span>Single model · Claude Opus 4.7</div>
        <div class="single-answer">
          <p>
            <strong>Stop. Or more precisely: stop the broad push and make a hard decision this week.</strong>
            60+ meetings with one soft commit is the market telling you clearly that this round, as currently
            packaged, isn't going to close.
          </p>
          <p>Do 5–10 honest post-mortem calls with investors who passed but engaged seriously.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel parliament-panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span class="dot green"></span>Parliament · Debate</div>
        <div class="turns">
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent proposer">Proposer</span>
            <p>
              Separate "is the business worth continuing" (customer signal) from "is this round worth
              continuing" (investor signal). Pause broad outreach, do honest diligence calls, look at
              runway.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              Investors often give polite or misleading feedback. Triangulate behavior, not stated
              reasons. Pausing outreach for a week could kill momentum if there are warm
              conversations active. Distinguish stopping broad top-of-funnel from aggressively
              closing real active leads.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent redagent">Red Agent</span>
            <p>
              The weakest assumption is that investor behavior after 60 meetings is reliable signal
              about the company itself. It isn't. <strong>Most investor conversations are noisy theater</strong>
              — polite deflections, shifting criteria, herding instincts that reveal more about the current
              mood of capital than about your traction. Continuing to push is endowment effect dressed
              up as persistence. Redirect every cycle into product and revenue immediately.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="turn">
            <span class="agent skeptic">Skeptic</span>
            <p>
              It's noisy but still meaningful signal about fundability under current conditions.
              Sixty meetings is large enough to warrant serious inference. The advice should define
              <strong>"genuine conviction"</strong> concretely: partner meetings, active diligence, terms
              discussion, reference calls — not polite encouragement.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="surfaced">
      <div class="surfaced-label">What surfaced</div>
      <p>
        The single-model answer says "stop." Parliament's answer is also "stop" — but the
        deliberation produces an <strong>operational definition of when to restart</strong> that no single
        answer offered: a relaunch should be gated on a <em>material</em> milestone (not cosmetic narrative
        changes), and "genuine conviction" in the active pipeline means partner meetings, active diligence,
        terms discussion, reference calls — not polite encouragement. The difference between a founder
        who flails for another quarter and one who knows exactly what evidence would change the call.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="closing">
    <h2>What you're paying for</h2>
    <p>A single frontier model is optimized to give you the best single answer.</p>
    <p>Parliament is optimized to surface what the best single answer would have to assume away.</p>
    <p class="pitch">
      For most questions, the single answer is enough.<br />
      For the questions where the discussion matters — where assumptions are the actual problem, where
      the stakes are large, where you have to defend the call later —
      <strong>Parliament gives you the transcript that shows your work.</strong>
    </p>
    <div class="closing-cta">
      <a href="https://github.com/heybeaux/parliament" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        >Start deliberating →</a
      >
      <a href={homeHref} class="btn btn-secondary">Back to overview</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="methodology">
    <p>
      The six examples above were drawn from a 60-question study comparing Parliament against Claude
      Opus 4.7, GPT-5.5, Gemini 3.1 Pro, and Grok 4.20 on advisory questions across logic traps,
      false premises, sunk-cost reasoning, and adversarial framings. Full data and methodology
      available on request.
    </p>
  </div>

  <footer>
    <div class="footer-content">
      <div class="footer-links">
        <a href="https://github.com/heybeaux/parliament" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/heybeaux/parliament/blob/main/docs/topology.md"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Docs</a
        >
        <a
          href="https://github.com/heybeaux/parliament/blob/main/docs/neurotypes.md"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Neurotypes</a
        >
        <a href="https://heybeaux.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">heybeaux.dev</a>
      </div>
      <p class="footer-copy">Built by heybeaux. Part of the reasoning layer — ACR + Engram + Parliament.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  :root {
    --bg: #0a0a0f;
    --bg-card: #12121a;
    --text: #e8e8ed;
    --text-muted: #8888a0;
    --accent: #6366f1;
    --accent-glow: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.3);
    --green: #22c55e;
    --amber: #f59e0b;
    --red: #ef4444;
    --border: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
  }

  * {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  html {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }

  body {
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text);
    line-height: 1.6;
    overflow-x: hidden;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 0.2s;
  }

  a:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .nav {
    padding: 1.5rem 2rem;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    max-width: 1100px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .nav-brand {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .nav-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .nav-links a {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .nav-links a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .showcase-hero {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 5rem 2rem 3rem;
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    position: relative;
  }
  .showcase-hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    width: 600px;
    height: 600px;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, var(--accent-glow) 0%, transparent 70%);
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: -1;
  }
  .showcase-hero .eyebrow {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.15em;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .showcase-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .showcase-hero h1 .accent {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-shadow: 0 0 40px var(--accent-glow);
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .showcase-hero p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: 0 auto 1rem;
  }
  .showcase-intro {
    max-width: 740px;
    margin: 0 auto 4rem;
    padding: 0 2rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .showcase-intro strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .example {
